            <ArticleTitle>Prone positioning reduces perineal infections when performing the miles procedure.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>217-221</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S0002-9610(16)31161-8</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.amjsurg.2017.05.021</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="BACKGROUND" NlmCategory="BACKGROUND">Abdominoperineal resection (APR) remains the cornerstone treatment for rectal cancers less than 5 cm from the anal verge. The perineal portion of an APR can be done with the patient in lithotomy or repositioned to prone jack-knife position, which influences accessibility, visualization and ability to close the wound. This paper analyses the effect of patient positioning on perineal wound dehiscence and infections.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">A retrospective review of all rectal cancer patients who underwent an APR at Massachusetts General Hospital between 2004 and 2014 (n = 149). Patients were divided into supine (n = 91) or prone (n = 58) positioning as documented in operative reports.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">Twenty-two percent of supine positioned patients developed a perineal wound infection, versus 3.4% of the prone patients (P = 0.002). Perineal wound dehiscence rate was also higher in the supine positioned group (14.3% vs. prone 3.4%; P = 0.032). Multivariable analysis showed OR = 9.2 of developing a perineal wound infection for supine positioned patients, compared to prone, corrected for obesity and smoking history.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Repositioning patients into prone position for the perineal portion of an APR was associated with significantly lower perineal wound infection and dehiscence rates compared to supine positioned patients.</AbstractText>
